import{_ as t,o,c as a,a2 as s}from"./chunks/framework.BN3Eol-0.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"ADR-096 — Indexable entity pages and the SEO surface","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/ADR-096.md","filePath":"adr/ADR-096.md"}'),r={name:"adr/ADR-096.md"};function n(d,e,i,c,l,h){return o(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="adr-096-—-indexable-entity-pages-and-the-seo-surface" tabindex="-1">ADR-096 — Indexable entity pages and the SEO surface <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-096-—-indexable-entity-pages-and-the-seo-surface" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;ADR-096 — Indexable entity pages and the SEO surface&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Status · Accepted · 2026-11-09 (operator; amended with decisions 8–12) · 0.9.8 plan item 0 (operator, 2026-11-08) · evidence in <code>docs/wip/2026-11-08-seo-review/</code> · builds on <strong>ADR-034</strong> (prerender, server-only loads) and the <code>#519</code> hreflang work · the <code>/c/*</code> share stubs of the collectible cards stay as they are</p></blockquote><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The 2026-11-08 SEO audit of the build found that search engines could rank almost none of what Orrery knows. Every mission, vehicle, landing site, body and Lab calculator lived only in a client-side panel (<code>/missions?id=</code>, <code>/fleet?id=</code>, <code>/moon?site=</code>, <code>/explore?id=</code>, the <code>/lab</code> picker); those URLs canonicalise to their hub, so the entity text never reached prerendered HTML. The only per-entity pages were the 523 <code>/c/*</code> card stubs — en-US, noindex, redirecting. Around that: no Open Graph or JSON-LD on any page, 476 pages without a description, 42 titled just &quot;Orrery&quot;, a 7 MB single-file sitemap, and the GitHub Pages staging mirror outranking the real site for &quot;porkchop plot calculator&quot; despite its canonical tags.</p><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><strong>One indexable page per entity, in every locale,</strong> prerendered from the data and overlays the panels already use — no new text, the panels&#39; own label messages: <code>/missions/&lt;id&gt;</code> (126), <code>/fleet/&lt;id&gt;</code> (294), <code>/moon|mars|venus/&lt;site&gt;</code> (58), <code>/explore/&lt;body&gt;</code> (48: planets, moons, small bodies), <code>/lab/&lt;formula&gt;</code> (65). × 14 locales = 8,302 pages. Each has H1, facts, the long text, links between entities (mission ↔ vehicle ↔ site), and an &quot;Open in Orrery&quot; link to the in-app view. The in-app panels stay the app; the pages are what search engines and shared links land on.</li><li><strong>Server-only loads</strong> (<code>+page.server.ts</code>) for every entity route: the JSON they read stays out of client JS (a universal load put <code>/fleet/[id]</code> at 51 KB against its 30 KB route budget), and loaders take the prerender <code>fetch</code> (<code>getFleet</code>, <code>getSiteStory</code>, new <code>getSiteEntity</code>, <code>getBodyEntity</code>). The Lab formula ids are mirrored in <code>static/data/lab-formulas.json</code> because <code>scripts/site-routes.mjs</code> (plain Node) cannot import the TS registry; a test pins it to <code>REGISTRY</code>.</li><li><strong>One route list</strong> — <code>scripts/site-routes.mjs</code> seeds the per-locale prerender and the sitemaps, so neither can drift. Sitemaps: <code>/sitemap.xml</code> is an index of 14 <code>/sitemaps/&lt;locale&gt;.xml</code>. Each URL carries a truthful <code>&lt;lastmod&gt;</code> from the committed <code>src/lib/generated/route-lastmod.json</code> (<code>npm run route-lastmod</code>, run in the release PR on a full-history checkout): the date git last changed the files that page is built from. It cannot be computed in the build: CI is a depth-1 checkout and <code>.git</code> is not in the Docker context, so every date would be the build commit&#39;s. A route missing from the manifest gets no <code>&lt;lastmod&gt;</code>.</li><li><strong>Head tags from one place each.</strong> <code>metaDescription()</code> (≤160, sentence cut) and <code>pageTitle()</code> (≤60, &quot;Topic — subtitle&quot; keeps the topic) in <code>$lib/seo</code>; Open Graph + Twitter tags are added to every page by <code>addSocialMeta()</code> in the prerender hook from the page&#39;s own title, description and canonical (a page&#39;s own <code>og:image</code> — mission / fleet cards — wins); JSON-LD via <code>&lt;JsonLd&gt;</code> (<code>Article</code> + <code>BreadcrumbList</code> on reading and entity pages, <code>WebSite</code> + <code>Organization</code> on home).</li><li><strong>The staging mirror is noindex</strong> (any build with a base path): no canonical, no hreflang.</li><li><strong>The gate.</strong> <code>scripts/check-seo.mjs</code> runs in every <code>npm run build</code>: per indexable page one title (not the bare brand, ≤90), one description (≤160), self canonical, complete hreflang with built targets, og/twitter tags, JSON-LD that parses, one H1, no noindex; sitemaps = indexable pages; on the mirror, every page noindex.</li><li><strong>Mobile ships none of it.</strong> <code>prune-streamed-assets.mjs</code> drops the entity pages and their <code>__data.json</code>, the sitemaps and the stubs (mobile build 75.6 MB of its 77 MB budget).</li></ol><p>Amended 2026-11-09 with the operator&#39;s decisions on the open questions:</p><ol start="8"><li><strong>Hub links in the footer.</strong> Explore · Worlds · Catalog · Learn on every page, with the nav&#39;s own labels: the four hub pages were linked only from TV mode and the sitemap.</li><li><strong>HTML stays uncached at the edge.</strong> Cloudflare caches <code>/_app/immutable</code> only; caching HTML would need a purge on every deploy, or cached pages point at deleted chunks. Revisit if Search Console shows slow time to first byte.</li><li><strong>Search titles beside poetic headings.</strong> Essays, programs, science sections and weak science articles carry <code>seo_title</code> / <code>seo_description</code> in their overlays (14 locales); <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, og and the meta description use them, the visible H1 and JSON-LD keep the poetic title.</li><li><strong>Every hub links all its entity pages in the prerendered HTML.</strong> <code>/missions</code> and <code>/fleet</code> prerender a collapsed A–Z list at the page foot (<code>EntityIndex</code>, server load, no client JS cost). On the scene routes (<code>/moon</code>, <code>/mars</code>, <code>/venus</code>, <code>/explore</code>) the links live in the INDEX panel (UXD-015, 2026-11-12: the separate A–Z tab is gone): the panel is always in the DOM, hidden while closed, and before the sites load it is the plain list of every entity page from the route&#39;s server load; once loaded, each row whose object has a page carries a real link. Panels open on a click, which crawlers don&#39;t make, so the links are in the markup, not behind the click. <code>tests/e2e/index-links.spec.ts</code> checks the static HTML of every hub. (Amended 2026-11-12, UXD-017: <code>/explore</code> has the same INDEX on all eight scales, one component (<code>ScaleIndex</code>); its eight lists are all in the one prerendered <code>/explore</code> HTML, hidden unless on screen, the rows with a page as plain links and the rows without one added on hydration. Explore&#39;s moons are rows of the solar-system list, not separate link rows. <code>tests/e2e/scale-index.spec.ts</code> checks the HTML and every scale.)</li><li><strong>Thin-page regression floor.</strong> All entity pages stay indexable; thin ones are enriched from localized data the panels already show or the data files hold (linked missions with their one-liners, sites, crew, museums, related hardware, orbit and size figures, science sections, a lab formula&#39;s dials, defaults, worked result and assumptions) — no new prose. <code>check-seo.mjs</code> then fails an entity page whose <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> text is under its locale&#39;s character floor (<code>THIN_MIN_CHARS</code>). Characters, not words, because words are not a unit that holds across languages; per locale because the same content is ~1.1-1.2x the English length in the Latin / Cyrillic locales, ~1.0x in hi / ar and ~0.55-0.6x in zh-CN / ja / ko. Each floor is 95 % of that locale&#39;s thinnest page after the enrichment (en-US 350, zh-CN 175), so it guards against regressions rather than judging content; <code>CHECK_SEO_FLOORS=1 node scripts/check-seo.mjs</code> re-measures it.</li></ol><p>Amended 2026-11-12 with the deep levels (operator: every deep type gets entity pages; stars and their exoplanet hosts first; brief <code>docs/wip/2026-11-12-deep-entity-pages-brief.md</code>):</p><ol start="13"><li><p><strong>Stars are entity pages, typed under <code>/explore/stars/&lt;id&gt;</code>.</strong> One page per named star (62) and per exoplanet host (11, seven of which are named stars): 66 pages × 14 locales. The four host-only stars (<code>trappist-1</code>, <code>51-pegasi</code>, <code>toi-700</code>, <code>kepler-16</code>) are built from the system record. The typed path keeps a star id from ever meeting a planet, moon or small-body id (<code>getEntityIndex(&#39;explore&#39;)</code> lists only single-segment ids), and <code>getStarEntity</code> (<code>$lib/data/star-entity</code>, server-only) is keyed by star id alone. <strong>An exoplanet system is a section of its host&#39;s page</strong> (<code>#system</code>, each planet an anchor with its overlay text and data rows, culture doors included), not a URL of its own. Text is what the panels already show (the star and exoplanet overlays, catalogue values, culture doors, the H–R article link): no new prose. The page links back into the scene with <code>/explore?goto=&lt;id&gt;</code>, or <code>/explore?system=&lt;id&gt;</code> for a host-only star, and each planet with <code>?system=&amp;planet=</code>. The thin-page floor (item 12) is unchanged and applies to these pages; a rendered measure of all 66 clears it in every locale. <strong>Hero (2026-11-13, operator: &quot;All 7&quot; after a side-by-side of the ten panel photos):</strong> <code>getStarEntity</code> carries <code>hero</code> (<code>getPageHero</code> over the overlay&#39;s <code>photo</code>, crop from <code>stars-hero-overrides.json</code>, as in the panel) and the page shows one <code>EntityHero</code> under the lede, the credit from the provenance row, the JSON-LD article carrying <code>image</code>. Seven stars have one (Sirius, Rigil Kentaurus, Rigel, Betelgeuse, Antares, Fomalhaut, Proxima Centauri); Vega (a labelled JWST disk figure with the star masked), Altair (a gridded model view) and Barnard&#39;s Star (an unmarked field) keep the panel image and get none (<code>NO_PAGE_HERO</code>: a constant, because <code>hero-overrides.schema.json</code> is shared by nine files and allows no extra key). Provenance rows corrected on the way: Altair is PD-self (the derivative author&#39;s release), not PD-Old; Proxima Centauri is CC-BY-4.0, not CC-BY-2.0.</p></li><li><p><strong>The scale INDEX is the crawl path; there is no list page.</strong> Each INDEX row carries a <code>↗</code> link to its page (30 px on a fine pointer, 44 px on a coarse one). <code>/explore</code> renders the INDEX of every scale, hidden unless on screen, with its rows from the server load (<code>getScaleIndexRows</code> in <code>$lib/server/entity-index</code>, which names every row as its page does), so the prerendered <code>/explore</code> HTML links every body, star and black-hole page. (Amended 2026-11-12, UXD-017: the first cut had a star INDEX at the neighbourhood and a body INDEX at the solar system; both are now <code>ScaleIndex</code>, and the six other scales have the same one.) Star and black-hole pages are reached like planets: from the INDEX of the scale they belong to, with the crumb back to <code>/explore</code>. (Amended 2026-11-12: the <code>/explore/stars</code> and <code>/explore/black-holes</code> list pages of the first cut were removed, operator: stars and black holes are members of the scale INDEX, not hub cards or lists of their own.) The mobile build prunes every star page with its <code>__data.json</code> (item 7).</p></li><li><p><strong>Remaining deep types.</strong> (Amended 2026-11-13: the first sentence of the first cut deferred the Local Group and the outer shells until each entity&#39;s text cleared the floor; the operator&#39;s rule of 2026-11-12 is that every entity has a panel, a deep link and a page, so the galaxies and the structures are now item 18.) The Milky Way&#39;s five spiral arms and the Sun stay INDEX rows; 47 Tucanae and NGC 6752 become deep-sky objects with text (a deep-sky change, item 17&#39;s set). Where the text is missing it is researched with cited sources, reviewed and translated by hand before the page ships; no page is shipped on a shared generic paragraph. Until then they stay INDEX rows that open their panels: since UXD-017 every one of them has its row in the INDEX of its scale before it has a page, and the page, when it ships, is one <code>href</code> on that row, no new UI. Black holes were the first of these to ship (item 16), the deep-sky objects with text the second (item 17).</p></li><li><p><strong>Black holes are entity pages, typed under <code>/explore/black-holes/&lt;id&gt;</code>.</strong> Four pages (<code>sagittarius-a-star</code>, <code>m87-star</code>, <code>cygnus-x-1</code>, <code>gargantua</code>) × 14 locales, built by <code>getBlackHoleEntity</code> (<code>$lib/data/black-hole-entity</code>, server-only). The text is the researched, science-reviewed <code>fact</code> and <code>bio</code> of <code>docs/wip/deep-text/batch-01.md</code> in <code>i18n-src/&lt;locale&gt;/universe/black-holes/&lt;id&gt;.json</code>, translated by hand into the 13 other locales with the glossary forms of <code>native-terms.json</code>, plus the <code>discovery</code> line (English-only in the data file until now), which the panel shows in the reader&#39;s language too. The sources are in <code>static/data/text-sources.json</code> (ESO eso1907 and eso2208, NASA Science, Miller-Jones et al. 2021, the DNGR paper, Thorne&#39;s APS interview, the GRAVITY 2022 mass) and on <code>/credits</code>. The page shows mass, distance and Schwarzschild radius of the three real holes and no spin (no source for any of the three); M87* takes the EHT image already in the repo as its hero. Mass and distance are the published values (GitHub #658): GRAVITY Collaboration 2022 for Sgr A*, EHT Collaboration 2019 paper VI for M87*, Miller-Jones et al. 2021 for Cygnus X-1, each row names its paper with a link, and a &quot;Data sources&quot; section lists the papers (ADS bibcode links) and the object&#39;s SIMBAD page (<code>scripts/refresh-black-hole-catalogue.mjs</code>). <strong>Gargantua is fiction</strong>: a visible badge (<code>bh_fiction_badge</code>), dashed and muted rather than in the accent colour, no figure at all (its mass, distance, spin and radius are model parameters with no source), nothing styled as a measurement. The row of Gargantua in the INDEX is dashed and muted the same way. M87*&#39;s distance is the EHT paper&#39;s adopted 16.8 Mpc, 54.8 million light-years (ESO&#39;s eso1907 round figure, 55 million, is the same distance; Cosmicflows-4 gives M87 55.0 million, noted in the data). The pages link back with <code>/explore?bh=&lt;id&gt;</code>; the rows of the black holes carry the <code>↗</code> link (item 14&#39;s pattern), each in the INDEX of the scale it belongs to (UXD-017: Sagittarius A* and Cygnus X-1 in the Milky Way, M87* in Virgo, Gargantua, fiction, in a &quot;Fiction&quot; section at the foot of the Cosmic web), the pages are in the sitemaps, and the mobile build prunes them (item 7). The thin-page floor (item 12) applies unchanged; a rendered measure clears it in every locale.</p></li><li><p><strong>Deep-sky objects are entity pages, typed under <code>/explore/deep-sky/&lt;id&gt;</code></strong> (amended 2026-11-12, operator: all eight recommendations of <code>docs/wip/2026-11-12-deep-sky-pages-brief.md</code> approved). One page per deep-sky object <strong>with reviewed text</strong>: the set is the ids of <code>i18n-src/en-US/universe/deep-sky/*.json</code> that are objects of <code>deep-sky-objects.json</code> (<code>deepSkyPageIds()</code> in <code>scripts/site-routes.mjs</code>), 120 of 141 today, × 14 locales = 1,680 pages. The files decide: a text batch (the other 21, all photo-backed) adds its pages by adding its overlay files, with no code change, and <code>validate-data</code> requires each overlay in all 14 locales with a <code>fact</code>, a <code>bio</code> and one or two links. Built by <code>getDeepSkyEntity</code> (<code>$lib/data/deep-sky-entity</code>, server-only): the catalogue record, the locale overlay (en-US fallback), the culture doors (M13, the Arecibo message) and, for a photographed object, the gallery telescope, caption and credit; an object with no overlay has no page. <strong>One real object, one page, under the deep-sky id</strong> (the only id with a record, an overlay, a photo and catalogue sources): the Milky Way globulars (<code>m3 m4 m5 m13 m15 m22 m92</code>) and the Local Group members <code>m32</code> and <code>m110</code> carry that id already, and <code>DEEP_SKY_ALIAS</code> (<code>andromeda → m31</code>, <code>triangulum → m33</code>, <code>omega-centauri → ngc-5139</code>, the last linked once ngc-5139 has text) names the others; their INDEX rows (UXD-017) link the same page and are named as the page names it. No second page, and no Milky Way or Local Group page for an object that has none (the other members stay INDEX rows). <strong>Title</strong> (brief Q5): designation, then the common name where the data has one (<code>M13 · Hercules Globular Cluster</code>, <code>M3 · NGC 5272</code>, <code>NGC 1365</code>); the data name is English in every locale and 88 of 141 are a bare OpenNGC code, so <code>deepSkyTitle</code> (<code>$lib/explore/deep-sky-title</code>) spaces the code and does not repeat it; the page H1, the <code>&lt;title&gt;</code>, the panel title and the INDEX row are that one string. The page shows the distance (from <code>dist_ly</code>, two significant figures from 10,000 ly up, the locale&#39;s number format and unit, <code>deepSkyDistance</code>), the V magnitude where there is one, the telescope of a photographed object and the constellation spelled out, each catalogue value linked to its source, the Data sources block (#658), the culture door, the overlay&#39;s learn-more links, the <code>gatewaySystem</code> link to the star page (<code>/explore/stars/&lt;host&gt;</code>), the gallery link and the credit of a photographed object, and the link back into the scene, <code>/explore?deepsky=&lt;id&gt;</code>. The panel and the page use the same formatters (<code>src/lib/explore/format.ts</code>). <strong>Select = focus (UXD-019) for every object</strong>: before this item only an object with a photo or a culture door opened its panel from <code>?deepsky=</code>, the INDEX or the canvas, and the other 96 with text were a highlighted dot; <code>enterDeepSky</code> now opens the flat panel, rings the glint and turns the camera to every one (a photo-less object has no approach warp and no hero). The pages are in the sitemaps and the mobile build prunes them (item 7). The thin-page floor (item 12) applies unchanged. <strong>Hero (2026-11-13, phase 4 of the galaxy brief):</strong> a photographed object&#39;s page shows its gallery photo as the hero (<code>EntityHero</code>, <code>getPageHero</code> in <code>$lib/data/universe</code>: the 700 px thumb with the full image as the wider srcset rung, alt text from <code>image-alt-text</code> in the reader&#39;s language, the author from the provenance row); an object without a photo takes the panel hero of the scene id that names it (<code>DEEP_SKY_ALIAS</code>: M33 shows NGC 604, captioned &quot;Pictured: NGC 604&quot;); otherwise no hero and no slot. The page emits no <code>og:image</code> of its own (the default share card stands); its JSON-LD article carries the hero as <code>image</code>.</p></li><li><p><strong>Galaxies and structures are entity pages, typed under <code>/explore/galaxies/&lt;id&gt;</code> and <code>/explore/structures/&lt;id&gt;</code></strong> (2026-11-13, operator: all eight recommendations of <code>docs/wip/2026-11-12-galaxy-pages-brief.md</code> approved; phase 1 built, no text yet). Two paths: the 29 Local Group galaxies that are not deep-sky twins (the Milky Way, the Large and Small Magellanic Clouds and 26 dwarfs) and the 30 structures of the Local Sheet, Virgo, Laniakea and Cosmic web files (9 groups, 5 clouds, 3 clusters, 7 superclusters, the Great Attractor, the Shapley Concentration, 3 walls, the Boötes Void), 59 pages × 14 locales = 826. <strong>The files decide</strong>, as for deep-sky: <code>galaxyPageIds()</code> and <code>structurePageIds()</code> in <code>scripts/site-routes.mjs</code> list the members of the data files that have an en-US overlay under <code>i18n-src/en-US/universe/galaxies/</code> or <code>…/structures/</code>, so until a text batch writes overlays there are no pages and the build&#39;s page set is unchanged; <code>validate-data</code> requires each overlay in all 14 locales with a <code>fact</code>, a <code>bio</code> and one or two links and an id that is a member of the right file. <code>svelte.config.js</code> carries a <code>handleUnseenRoutes</code> that tolerates the two empty page types only while their list is empty. <strong>One real object, one page:</strong> M31, M33, M32 and M110 keep <code>/explore/deep-sky/&lt;id&gt;</code> (<code>andromeda</code> and <code>triangulum</code> are <code>DEEP_SKY_ALIAS</code>, <code>m32</code> and <code>m110</code> carry the deep-sky id), and their INDEX rows link it; <code>local-group</code> is a member of two scale files and has one structure page, <code>lmc</code> and <code>smc</code> are Local Group members and Milky Way pins and have one galaxy page. <strong>Structures are entities</strong> (including the four that no catalogue holds, the void and the walls): the page says what the structure is and what is real (distance, extent) and links its members; the schematic positions are never stated in the text. Built by <code>getGalaxyEntity</code> and <code>getStructureEntity</code> (<code>$lib/data/outer-entity</code>, server-only) and one template, <code>OuterEntityPage</code>: the overlay&#39;s fact (lede) and bio, rows (membership, distance, diameter or span), the Data sources block for a catalogue distance (a galaxy&#39;s; a structure&#39;s where the batch 06 review read a paper that states it, 15 of 30, <code>scripts/lib/structure-distance-catalogue.mjs</code>), the learn-more links, the JSON-LD article and breadcrumbs, and the link into the scene, <code>/explore?context=&lt;scale&gt;&amp;pick=&lt;id&gt;</code> (UXD-019; <code>local-sheet</code> for <code>local-group</code>, <code>laniakea</code> for the Virgo Supercluster, <code>cosmic-web</code> for Laniakea: the scale where the entity is itself the object). <strong>A Related list computed from the data</strong>, not prose (three message keys, <code>explore_related_part_of</code>, <code>_members</code>, <code>_same_scale</code>): the host galaxy and the Local Group for a galaxy, a galaxy&#39;s satellites; for a structure, the structure its scale is the view of (Virgo → the Virgo Supercluster, Laniakea → Laniakea), the members that have a page (the Local Group lists its galaxies, a twin under its deep-sky page) and the other members of its scale. A link appears only where its page exists. The panel and the page share their formatters (<code>galaxyDistance</code>, <code>structureDistance</code>, <code>structureSpan</code>, <code>galaxyGroupLabel</code> in <code>src/lib/explore/format.ts</code>); the six outer panels show the same overlay fact and bio as the page (their kind blurb only where no overlay exists), and a Magellanic Cloud&#39;s Milky Way pin prints its Local Group strings (<code>galaxyFacts</code>). &quot;Part of&quot; is shown only where the reviewed text says it (an explicit list in <code>outer-entity.ts</code>). The INDEX rows of all five scales link a page through one resolver (<code>outerPageOf</code> in <code>scale-index.ts</code>, the deep-sky resolver first). Names are the glossary&#39;s per language (<code>getOuterNames</code>; one string for the page H1, the panel title and the INDEX row); the four deep-sky twins keep their deep-sky names. Shipped 2026-11-13 (deep-text batches 05 and 06, 59 overlays × 14 locales, 826 pages; the build&#39;s temporary <code>handleUnseenRoutes</code> guard for the empty lists is gone). The mobile build prunes both folders (item 7), <code>check-seo</code> counts both under the thin-page floor (item 12), <code>build-route-lastmod</code> dates both from their census entry and overlay. <strong>Hero (2026-11-13, phase 4):</strong> a page shows the picture its panel shows, through the same loader (<code>panel-heroes.json</code> → <code>getUniverseHero</code> → <code>getPageHero</code>, one <code>EntityHero</code> figure under the lede): the LMC and SMC (Tarantula Nebula, NGC 346), IC 342 Group, M101 Group and the Perseus–Pisces Supercluster, each a photo of a part or member of the entity and captioned &quot;Pictured: …&quot;; every other galaxy and structure has none (no hero, no slot). No new image file; every image has a provenance row, so <code>/credits</code> lists it.</p></li></ol><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Positive.</strong> 12,138 indexable pages (was 3,892; items 13–14 add 924: 66 star pages × 14; item 16 adds 56: four black-hole pages × 14; item 17 adds 1,680: 120 deep-sky pages × 14, so 13,818 with it; item 18 adds 826: 59 galaxy and structure pages × 14, so 14,644 with it; the build counts 16,457 pages, 15,932 indexable, at 1771b675ec8), each with its own text, title, description, share card and structured data; every entity reachable by a stable URL in its reader&#39;s language; regressions fail the build instead of surfacing in Search Console weeks later.</p><p><strong>Negative.</strong> The web build grows by ~212 MB of HTML + <code>__data.json</code> and the prerender by a few minutes; the staging (GitHub Pages) publish carries it too. Some entity pages stay short where the data is thin; after the enrichment the shortest is 369 characters in en-US (<code>lab/weight</code>) and 27 of 8,303 were still under the old 80-word floor (mostly ko / sr-Cyrl, whose words are shorter or longer than English ones). Two views of every entity now exist; a change to what a panel shows should be mirrored on its page.</p><p><strong>Neutral.</strong> The panels do not link to the pages (decision 11 links them from the hubs instead); program rosters keep their <code>?id=</code> links into the 3D views.</p><h2 id="alternatives-considered" tabindex="-1">Alternatives considered <a class="header-anchor" href="#alternatives-considered" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Alternatives considered&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Make the <code>/c/*</code> stubs indexable.</strong> They redirect humans away instantly (Google treats that as a doorway / soft 404) and are en-US only. Rejected.</li><li><strong>Render the panels server-side on the hub routes</strong> (<code>/missions?id=</code> as one URL per entity). Query-string variants of one route are weak canonicals, and the hubs are 3D / client-heavy pages. Rejected.</li><li><strong>Hand-written SEO copy per entity.</strong> ~8,000 pages × 14 locales of new prose is a translation programme, not a change; the existing overlays already carry the text. Deferred: per-page SEO titles beside the poetic H1s of essays / programs / science need the operator&#39;s per-page yes.</li><li><strong>A dedicated JS route list for Lab formulas generated at build.</strong> A new build step for 65 ids; the guarded JSON file is smaller and fails loudly when it drifts.</li></ul>',16)])])}const u=t(r,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,u as default};
